{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"A series-trust platform is usually the lower-fixed-cost route for a first ETF: it can provide an established trust, independent board, and service-provider infrastructure, reducing setup work and ongoing governance burden. Compare the platform’s fees and minimums with the sponsor’s expected scale, and scrutinize its service levels, launch timeline, termination or transition terms, and how much flexibility it allows.\n\nBuilding a proprietary trust offers greater control over governance, policies, provider selection, branding, and future product development—but requires more legal and operational setup, board and compliance support, and recurring expense. For one ordinary ETF, those fixed costs may be hard to justify unless the sponsor expects meaningful scale or values independence enough to pay for it.\n\nThe trade-off is not simply “cheap versus control”: a platform can constrain decisions and create reliance on shared infrastructure, while a standalone trust gives control but also puts the burden of operating and overseeing that infrastructure on the sponsor. Model total costs and operational responsibilities under both paths, then choose based on scale, control priorities, and growth plans."}}
